WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.460
Carrie Fisher's daughter played a much bigger
role in The Rise of Skywalker than you might

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:04.960
think.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.780
After Fisher suddenly passed away from a heart
attack in December 2016, director J.J. Abrams

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.751
and the team behind The Rise of Skywalker
were left with a unique challenge: how could

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:19.140
they incorporate Fisher's iconic character
General Leia Organa without the actress?

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:23.940
Through some clever CGI tricks and a performer
capable of following in Fisher's footsteps,

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.640
the impossible became possible.

00:00:25.849 --> 00:00:30.340
Unbeknownst to most, Billie Lourd, who appeared
in The Force Awakens and The Rise of Skywalker

00:00:30.349 --> 00:00:35.320
as junior communications coordinator Lieutenant
Kaydel Ko Connix, also made an appearance

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.809
in the final film of the Skywalker Saga as
a young Leia during a crucial scene where

00:00:39.809 --> 00:00:42.640
the princess-turned-general trained in the
ways of the Force.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:48.000
In an interview with Yahoo!, visual effects
supervisor Patrick Tubach confirmed that while

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:52.260
Mark Hamill went through a de-aging process
to look like a younger Luke Skywalker, Lourd

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.399
stepped in to play Leia.

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:57.380
Rumors swirled for months that Lourd, whose
Lieutenant Connix sports Leia's signature

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:01.570
buns during her appearances on screen, would
be playing a young Leia at some point during

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:02.780
The Rise of Skywalker.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.800
Now, Tubach has confirmed those theories.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:09.160
Though Lourd's face isn't actually used in
the final product it's a CGI rendering of

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.740
what Fisher looked like in her youth everything
else of her physical form is.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:13.260
He said:

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:14.840
"Billie was playing her mother.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:19.031
It was a poignant thing, and something that
nobody took lightly that she was willing to

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.260
stand in for her mom [...] It was an emotional
thing for everybody to see her in that position.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:24.800
It felt great for us, too.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:28.600
If you're going to have someone play [Fisher's]
part, it's great that it's [Billie] because

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.360
there are a lot of similarities between them
that we were able to draw from.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:36.320
The real challenge was just making the Leia
footage we had to work with fit in that scene."

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.510
VFX supervisor Roger Guyett also told Yahoo:

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:41.170
"What you see is what we developed.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:44.810
The idea was to provide this great surprise
where they take the helmets off, and you see

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.560
Luke and Leia's younger faces.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:50.670
We scoured outtakes from the original movies,
and we took some pieces and then had to try

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.770
and figure out the technical aspect of putting
that shot together."

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:58.280
Going forward, whether or not Leia appears
in any future Star Wars projects will be entirely

00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.340
up to the young actress.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:00.600
As Guyett noted:

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.600
"The truth is that Carrie was a friend of
J.J. and Billie is a friend of J.J.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:08.259
They talked a lot about that, and I think
the heart of it is the utmost respect for

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.420
Carrie and her memory and her performance."

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:14.580
It couldn't have been easy for Lourd, who
lost both her beloved mother and her grandmother,

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:18.650
Debbie Reynolds, before 2016 came to an end,
to step in and play her mother.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:22.590
In the build-up to The Rise of Skywalker,
Lourd even addressed the legacy of both her

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:27.570
mother and Leia by penning a deeply emotional
essay for TIME Magazine where she discussed

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:30.720
the good and bad impact that Princess Leia
had on her life.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:35.320
Initially, Lourd was confused and often irritated
by her mother's fame, but eventually, she

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.060
came around.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:36.740
She wrote:

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.420
"I realized then that Leia is more than just
a character.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:40.600
She's a feeling.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:41.100
She is strength.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:42.360
She is grace.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:43.100
She is wit.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:44.840
She is femininity at its finest.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:46.880
She knows what she wants, and she gets it.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.860
She doesn't need anyone to defend her, because
she defends herself.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.900
And no one could have played her like my mother.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.560
Princess Leia is Carrie Fisher.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.420
Carrie Fisher is Princess Leia.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:56.760
The two go hand in hand."

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.910
Lourd then concluded the essay by saying:

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.680
"I grew up with three parents: a mom, a dad,
and Princess Leia.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.430
Initially, Princess Leia was kind of like
my stepmom.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.040
Now she's my guardian angel.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:08.000
And I'm her keeper."

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:12.260
Regardless of whether Lourd takes on the role
of Leia in a future Star Wars film, she's

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.520
got an undoubtedly bright future ahead of
her.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:19.080
After gaining fame and acclaim thanks to her
various roles on Ryan Murphy's anthology series

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.680
American Horror Story, Lourd made her mark
on the big screen in Olivia Wilde's directorial

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.780
debut Booksmart, playing the absurd, scene-stealing
character Gigi.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:32.319
If Lourd chooses to retire her mother's signature
character after The Rise of Skywalker, it's

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:36.460
a comforting relief for many fans to get even
a brief glimpse of this beloved character.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.100
And in the end, nobody else could have played
Leia quite like Lourd.

